\title{Integer Solutions of $y^{2}=(36n^{3}-19-12mn)^{2}-(2m)^{3}$:\\[4pt]
Complete Results to $|m|\le 10^{11}$, the Torsion-Translate\\
Correspondence, and the Mordell--Weil Structure of the Fibration}
\author{J.~Agbanwa\\\small with computational assistance from Claude (Anthropic)}
\date{June 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the Diophantine equation
\begin{equation*}
y^{2}=(36n^{3}-19-12mn)^{2}-(2m)^{3}
\end{equation*}
in integers $(n,m,y)$ with $m\neq0$. Starting from a table of $29$ known
nontrivial solution triples $(n,m,|y|)$, an exhaustive divisor-pair search
over $1\le|m|\le10^{11}$ (with $n$ unbounded) together with a
torsion-translate correspondence yields $29$ further triples, for a total of
$\mathbf{58}$ triples ($\mathbf{116}$ signed solutions). We establish:
(i)~an intrinsic bound $m\le24n^{2}$ for positive $m$, giving a complete
classification of positive-$m$ solutions for all $|n|\le64{,}549$;
(ii)~a stratified completeness result to $|m|\le10^{10}$ for unbalanced
divisor pairs;
(iii)~an explicit order-$3$ torsion section $T=(0,c)$ on every fiber whose
translation action pairs six fibers' solutions and generates the two largest
known solutions from modest in-range ones; and
(iv)~exact Mordell--Weil ranks for $22$ fibers, including one of rank~$3$.
The known range of $n$ grew from $[-1641,\,5118]$ to $[-53316,\,167163]$,
with the first solutions ever found satisfying $|n|>10^{5}$.
\end{abstract}

